  "gloss": "want, can, must, may",
  "title": "mau, bisa, harus, boleh",
  "pattern": "mau / bisa / harus / boleh + verb · Boleh saya …?",
  "explanation": "Four small words go before a verb, as in English: mau (want to), bisa (can), harus (must, have to) and boleh (may): Saya mau pulang (I want to go home), Dia bisa masak (She can cook). The verb after them never changes, and 'not' goes in front: tidak bisa (can't), tidak mau (don't want to). Mau is also the everyday 'going to', for what you're about to do: Saya mau mandi (I'm going to have a shower). To ask permission, start with boleh: Boleh saya duduk di sini? (May I sit here?)",
  "natural": "Bisa …? is the everyday 'Could you …?' and 'Can I …?': Bisa tunggu sebentar? (Could you wait a moment?), Bisa bayar pakai kartu? (Can I pay by card?). Answer with the same word: Boleh? — Boleh. Bisa? — Bisa.",
  "stiff": "Ingin is the more formal, written mau: at a warung, Saya ingin memesan sounds stiff, and Saya mau pesan is what people say. Harus aimed at someone older can sound bossy; soften it with sebaiknya (it's best to): Sebaiknya Ibu istirahat (You should rest).",
